---
import Note from '../../../layouts/Note.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import QA from '../../../components/QA.astro';
import Figure from '../../../components/Figure.astro';
import Math from '../../../components/Math.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'plain', label: 'In plain words' },
  { id: 'neuron', label: 'One neuron' },
  { id: 'activation', label: 'Why the bend matters' },
  { id: 'network', label: 'From neuron to network' },
  { id: 'code', label: 'Math and code' },
  { id: 'llm', label: 'Where this lives in an LLM' },
  { id: 'interview', label: 'Say it in the interview' },
  { id: 'questions', label: 'Practice questions' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Common mistakes' },
  { id: 'sources', label: 'Sources' },
  { id: 'revision', label: 'One-minute revision' },
];

// Fig. 3: a 3-4-2 network. Node positions per layer, then every connection between neighbours.
const layers = [
  { x: 120, ys: [70, 150, 230], fill: 'fill-gold' },
  { x: 360, ys: [50, 117, 183, 250], fill: 'fill-green' },
  { x: 600, ys: [110, 190], fill: 'fill-blue' },
];
const edges = layers.slice(0, -1).flatMap((from, i) =>
  from.ys.flatMap((y1) => layers[i + 1].ys.map((y2) => ({ x1: from.x + 22, y1, x2: layers[i + 1].x - 22, y2 })))
);

// Fig. 2: activation curves, sampled into SVG paths. 20px per unit on x.
const erf = (x: number) => {
  const t = 1 / (1 + 0.3275911 * Math_.abs(x));
  const y = 1 - ((((1.061405429 * t - 1.453152027) * t + 1.421413741) * t - 0.284496736) * t + 0.254829592) * t * Math_.exp(-x * x);
  return x >= 0 ? y : -y;
};
const Math_ = globalThis.Math;
const curve = (cx: number, cy: number, yScale: number, f: (x: number) => number) =>
  Array.from({ length: 81 }, (_, i) => {
    const x = -4 + i * 0.1;
    return `${i === 0 ? 'M' : 'L'}${(cx + x * 20).toFixed(1)} ${(cy - f(x) * yScale).toFixed(1)}`;
  }).join(' ');
const plots = [
  { cx: 120, name: 'ReLU', formula: 'max(0, z)', d: curve(120, 150, 20, (x) => Math_.max(0, x)) },
  { cx: 360, name: 'Sigmoid', formula: 'squashes into 0…1', d: curve(360, 150, 80, (x) => 1 / (1 + Math_.exp(-x))) },
  { cx: 600, name: 'GELU', formula: 'a smooth ReLU (used in GPT)', d: curve(600, 150, 20, (x) => (x * (1 + erf(x / Math_.SQRT2))) / 2) },
];

const numpyCode = `import numpy as np

x = np.array([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])        # three inputs
w = np.array([0.5, -1.0, 2.0])       # one weight per input
b = 0.1                              # bias

z = np.dot(w, x) + b                 # weighted sum: 0.5 - 2.0 + 6.0 + 0.1 = 4.6
a = max(0.0, z)                      # ReLU activation: 4.6
print(z, a)`;

const torchCode = `import torch
import torch.nn as nn

model = nn.Sequential(
    nn.Linear(3, 4),   # 3 inputs -> 4 hidden neurons: 3*4 weights + 4 biases = 16
    nn.ReLU(),         # the bend; has no parameters
    nn.Linear(4, 2),   # 4 hidden -> 2 outputs:        4*2 weights + 2 biases = 10
)

x = torch.tensor([[1.0, 2.0, 3.0]])   # shape (1, 3): a batch of one example
y = model(x)                          # the forward pass
print(y.shape)                        # torch.Size([1, 2])

print(sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters()))   # 26`;
---

<Note
  phase="foundations"
  slug="what-is-a-neural-network"
  lede="A neural network is a big adjustable formula. This note explains its smallest part, the neuron, and how stacking neurons makes something that can learn almost any pattern."
  sections={sections}
>
  <h2 id="plain">In plain words</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      Imagine deciding whether to go for a walk. You look at a few things: is it sunny, is it cold, are you tired? Each
      one matters a different amount. Sunshine pushes you towards “yes” strongly, tiredness pushes towards “no”. You
      add up all the pushes, and if the total is high enough, you go.
    </p>
    <p>
      A neuron does exactly this with numbers. A neural network is thousands or billions of these tiny deciders wired
      together, where the “how much each thing matters” numbers are not set by a person. The network finds them itself
      by looking at examples.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    The technical names: the things you look at are <span class="term">inputs</span> (or features), how much each
    matters is a <span class="term">weight</span>, your general willingness to say yes is the
    <span class="term">bias</span>, and the “is the total high enough?” step is the
    <span class="term">activation function</span>. Weights and biases together are the model’s
    <span class="term">parameters</span>. Finding good values for them from examples is called
    <span class="term">training</span>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="neuron">One neuron</h2>
  <Figure n={1} caption="A single neuron: multiply each input by its weight, add them up with the bias, then pass the total through an activation function.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of one neuron with three weighted inputs, a sum and an activation function">
      <g class="sketch">
        <path class="stroke" d="M95 70 L288 135" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M95 150 L280 150" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M95 230 L288 165" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <circle class="stroke fill-green" cx="340" cy="150" r="55"></circle>
        <path class="stroke" d="M395 150 H462" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <rect class="stroke fill-gold" x="470" y="112" width="96" height="76" rx="6"></rect>
        <path class="stroke ink-red" d="M488 170 H518 L548 128"></path>
        <path class="stroke" d="M566 150 H632" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
        <path class="stroke-soft" d="M340 50 V88" marker-end="url(#arrow)"></path>
      </g>
      <text x="70" y="77" text-anchor="middle">x₁</text>
      <text x="70" y="157" text-anchor="middle">x₂</text>
      <text x="70" y="237" text-anchor="middle">x₃</text>
      <text class="label-blue" x="180" y="88">w₁</text>
      <text class="label-blue" x="180" y="142">w₂</text>
      <text class="label-blue" x="180" y="222">w₃</text>
      <text x="340" y="146" text-anchor="middle">w·x + b</text>
      <text class="small" x="340" y="170" text-anchor="middle">= z</text>
      <text class="label-blue" x="340" y="40" text-anchor="middle">bias b</text>
      <text x="660" y="157" text-anchor="middle">a</text>
      <text class="small" x="340" y="240" text-anchor="middle">weighted sum</text>
      <text class="small" x="518" y="216" text-anchor="middle">activation f</text>
      <text class="small" x="660" y="182" text-anchor="middle">output</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="340" y="280" text-anchor="middle">blue = learned during training</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>

  <p>A neuron does two steps.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Weighted sum.</strong> Multiply every input by its weight, add the results, add the bias. The total is
      called the <span class="term">pre-activation</span>, written <Math tex="z" />.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Activation.</strong> Pass <Math tex="z" /> through a simple fixed function <Math tex="f" /> that bends it.
      The result <Math tex="a" /> is the neuron’s output.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <Math block tex="z = w_1x_1 + w_2x_2 + \dots + w_nx_n + b = \mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b \qquad a = f(z)" />
  <p>
    A large positive weight means “this input pushes the output up a lot”. A negative weight means it pushes the output
    down. A weight near zero means the neuron ignores that input. The bias shifts the whole sum up or down, so the
    neuron can be easy or hard to switch on regardless of the inputs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="activation">Why the bend matters</h2>
  <Callout kind="plain">
    <p>
      With only straight rulers you can draw only straight lines, no matter how many rulers you join end to end. To
      draw a curve you need something that bends. The activation function is the bend.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    The weighted sum is a <span class="term">linear</span> operation. If you stack linear operations with nothing in
    between, the result is still one linear operation. Two layers without activations,
  </p>
  <Math block tex="W_2(W_1\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b}_1) + \mathbf{b}_2 = (W_2W_1)\,\mathbf{x} + (W_2\mathbf{b}_1 + \mathbf{b}_2)," />
  <p>
    collapse into a single layer with weights <Math tex="W_2W_1" />. So a hundred-layer network without activations is
    no more powerful than one layer. The activation function adds <span class="term">non-linearity</span>, and that is
    what lets depth build complicated shapes out of simple pieces.
  </p>

  <Figure n={2} caption="Three common activation functions. Each takes the weighted sum z on the horizontal axis and returns the neuron’s output." wide>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 260" role="img" aria-label="Plots of ReLU, sigmoid and GELU activation functions">
      {
        plots.map((p) => (
          <>
            <g class="sketch">
              <path class="stroke-soft" d={`M${p.cx - 85} 150 H${p.cx + 85} M${p.cx} 60 V190`} />
              <path class="stroke ink-red" d={p.d} stroke-width="3" />
            </g>
            <text x={p.cx} y="38" text-anchor="middle">
              {p.name}
            </text>
            <text class="small" x={p.cx} y="230" text-anchor="middle">
              {p.formula}
            </text>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </svg>
  </Figure>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Activation</th><th>Formula</th><th>Where you meet it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>ReLU</td>
          <td><Math tex="\max(0, z)" /></td>
          <td>The default for most networks: cheap, and its slope is 1 for positive inputs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sigmoid</td>
          <td><Math tex="\dfrac{1}{1 + e^{-z}}" /></td>
          <td>Output layers that need a probability between 0 and 1. Rare in hidden layers today.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Tanh</td>
          <td><Math tex="\tanh(z)" /></td>
          <td>Like sigmoid but from −1 to 1. Common in older recurrent networks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>GELU</td>
          <td><Math tex="z\,\Phi(z)" /></td>
          <td>GPT-2, BERT and many transformers. A smooth version of ReLU.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="network">From neuron to network</h2>
  <p>
    Put several neurons side by side, all reading the same inputs, and you have a <span class="term">layer</span>.
    Feed one layer’s outputs into the next and you have a network. When every neuron connects to every output of the
    layer before it, the layer is called <span class="term">fully connected</span> (also “dense” or “linear”), and the
    whole thing is a <span class="term">multilayer perceptron (MLP)</span> or
    <span class="term">feed-forward network</span>.
  </p>

  <Figure n={3} caption="A small network with 3 inputs, one hidden layer of 4 neurons and 2 outputs. Every line is one weight.">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 340" role="img" aria-label="Fully connected network with layers of 3, 4 and 2 nodes">
      <g class="sketch">
        {edges.map((e) => <line class="stroke-soft" {...e} />)}
        {layers.map((l) => l.ys.map((y) => <circle class={`stroke ${l.fill}`} cx={l.x} cy={y} r="22" />))}
      </g>
      <text x="120" y="300" text-anchor="middle">input layer</text>
      <text x="360" y="300" text-anchor="middle">hidden layer</text>
      <text x="600" y="300" text-anchor="middle">output layer</text>
      <text class="small" x="120" y="324" text-anchor="middle">3 numbers in</text>
      <text class="small" x="360" y="324" text-anchor="middle">4 neurons</text>
      <text class="small" x="600" y="324" text-anchor="middle">2 numbers out</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="240" y="26" text-anchor="middle">3 × 4 = 12 weights</text>
      <text class="small label-red" x="480" y="26" text-anchor="middle">4 × 2 = 8 weights</text>
    </svg>
  </Figure>

  <ul>
    <li>
      The <strong>input layer</strong> is just the data, written as a list of numbers (a
      <span class="term">vector</span>). It does no computing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hidden layers</strong> sit in the middle. Early ones pick up simple patterns, later ones combine them
      into more abstract ones. “Deep” learning means many hidden layers.
    </li>
    <li>The <strong>output layer</strong> gives the answer, for example one score per possible class.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Running data through the network from input to output is called the <span class="term">forward pass</span>. Because
    a whole layer does the same thing for each of its neurons, we write it with a
    <span class="term">weight matrix</span> <Math tex="W" /> (one row per neuron) and a bias vector:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\mathbf{a} = f(W\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b})" />
  <p>
    This is why deep learning runs on matrix multiplication, and why GPUs, which are built to multiply matrices fast,
    matter so much.
  </p>

  <h3>Counting parameters</h3>
  <p>
    A fully connected layer with <Math tex="n" /> inputs and <Math tex="m" /> neurons has
    <Math tex="m \times n" /> weights and <Math tex="m" /> biases. For the network in Fig. 3:
  </p>
  <Math block tex="\underbrace{(3\times4 + 4)}_{\text{hidden: }16} + \underbrace{(4\times2 + 2)}_{\text{output: }10} = 26 \text{ parameters}" />
  <p>
    When people say GPT-2 small has 124 million parameters, they mean the same thing: 124 million individual numbers
    like these, all adjusted during training.
  </p>

  <h2 id="code">Math and code</h2>
  <p>One neuron with plain NumPy, matching Fig. 1:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={numpyCode} />
  <p>The network from Fig. 3 in PyTorch:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={torchCode} />
  <p>
    <code>nn.Linear(3, 4)</code> stores a weight matrix of shape <code>(4, 3)</code> and a bias of shape
    <code>(4,)</code>, and computes <Math tex="W\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b}" />. The weights start as small random numbers,
    so the output is meaningless until the network is trained. Training is the subject of the next two notes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="llm">Where this lives in an LLM</h2>
  <p>
    This is not only history. Every transformer block inside GPT contains a two-layer feed-forward network of exactly
    this kind: a linear layer, a GELU activation, and another linear layer. In GPT-2 small the first of those layers
    goes from 768 numbers to 3,072, which is
    <Math tex="768 \times 3072 + 3072 = 2{,}362{,}368" /> parameters in one layer. About two thirds of a GPT model’s
    parameters sit in these feed-forward layers. Attention, which you will meet in Phase 2, is the other third.
  </p>

  <h2 id="interview">Say it in the interview</h2>
  <Callout kind="interview">
    <p>
      “A neural network is a parameterized function built from layers. Each layer computes a linear transformation of
      its input, a weight matrix times the input plus a bias, and then applies a non-linear activation function such as
      ReLU or GELU. The weights and biases are the learnable parameters; they start random and are adjusted during
      training to minimize a loss.
    </p>
    <p>
      The non-linearity is essential: without it, any stack of layers collapses into a single linear transformation, so
      depth would add nothing. With it, the network can approximate very complex functions, with early layers learning
      simple features and later layers composing them. The same building block appears in transformers as the
      feed-forward sub-layer of every block.”
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="questions">Practice questions</h2>
  <p>Answer each one out loud before opening it.</p>

  <QA q="Why do neural networks need activation functions?">
    <p>
      To introduce non-linearity. A composition of linear functions is itself linear, so without activations a deep
      network is equivalent to a single linear layer and can only model straight-line relationships. Non-linear
      activations let stacked layers represent curved, complex decision boundaries.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is the difference between a weight and a bias?">
    <p>
      A weight scales one input and controls how strongly that input influences the neuron. The bias is a constant
      added to the weighted sum that does not depend on any input; it shifts the activation threshold. Without a bias,
      the neuron’s output for an all-zero input would be fixed, and the function it draws would be forced through the
      origin.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is the difference between a parameter and a hyperparameter?">
    <p>
      Parameters are learned from data during training: the weights and biases. Hyperparameters are chosen by the
      engineer before training and are not learned: the number of layers, the layer width, the learning rate, the batch
      size, the choice of activation.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="How many parameters does a linear layer from 768 to 3,072 units have?">
    <p>
      Weights: 768 × 3,072 = 2,359,296. Biases: 3,072. Total: 2,362,368. The general rule is inputs × outputs + outputs.
      This particular layer is the first half of the feed-forward block in GPT-2 small.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why is ReLU usually preferred to sigmoid in hidden layers?">
    <p>
      Sigmoid saturates: for large positive or negative inputs its slope is almost zero, and its slope is never more
      than 0.25. During backpropagation those small slopes multiply across layers and the gradient shrinks towards
      zero, the <strong>vanishing gradient</strong> problem, so early layers stop learning. ReLU has a slope of exactly
      1 for positive inputs, which keeps gradients alive, and it is cheaper to compute. Its weakness is “dying ReLU”: a
      neuron whose input is always negative outputs zero and gets no gradient. Variants such as Leaky ReLU and GELU
      address this.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What does the universal approximation theorem say, and what does it not say?">
    <p>
      It says a feed-forward network with a single hidden layer and a suitable non-linear activation can approximate
      any continuous function on a bounded input region as closely as you like, given enough hidden neurons. It does
      not say how many neurons are needed (possibly an impractical number), nor that training will actually find the
      right weights, nor that the result will generalize to new data.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="If one wide layer is enough in theory, why do we use deep networks?">
    <p>
      Depth is far more parameter-efficient. Deep networks build features in a hierarchy, reusing simple features to
      form complex ones, so they can represent with a modest number of parameters what a shallow network would need
      exponentially many neurons for. In practice deep networks also generalize better on structured data such as
      images and language.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is a forward pass?">
    <p>
      Computing the network’s output for a given input by applying each layer in order, from the input layer to the
      output layer. It is what happens at inference time, and it is the first half of every training step; the second
      half is the backward pass, which computes gradients.
    </p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>“The activation function is what the network learns.”</strong> No. The activation is a fixed function
      with nothing to learn. The network learns the weights and biases.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Counting the input layer as a layer of neurons.</strong> The input layer holds data and has no
      parameters. The network in Fig. 3 has two layers of weights, and is usually called a two-layer network.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Forgetting the biases when counting parameters.</strong> It is inputs × outputs, plus one bias per
      output.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Saying networks “work like the brain”.</strong> The name is a loose historical analogy. In an interview,
      describe a network as a parameterized function trained by gradient descent.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>3Blue1Brown, <em>Neural Networks</em>, chapter 1: “But what is a neural network?” (video).</li>
    <li>Andrej Karpathy, <em>The spelled-out intro to neural networks and backpropagation: building micrograd</em> (video).</li>
    <li>Sebastian Raschka, <em>Build a Large Language Model (From Scratch)</em>, Appendix A, and chapter 4 for the feed-forward block and GELU.</li>
    <li>Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, <em>Deep Learning</em>, chapter 6: “Deep Feedforward Networks”.</li>
    <li>Hendrycks and Gimpel, “Gaussian Error Linear Units (GELUs)”, 2016.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="revision">One-minute revision</h2>
  <Callout kind="revision">
    <ul>
      <li>Neuron: <Math tex="a = f(\mathbf{w}\cdot\mathbf{x} + b)" />. Weighted sum, then activation.</li>
      <li>Layer: <Math tex="\mathbf{a} = f(W\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{b})" />. Parameters = inputs × outputs + outputs.</li>
      <li>Parameters (weights, biases) are learned. Hyperparameters (depth, width, learning rate) are chosen.</li>
      <li>No activation → the whole stack collapses to one linear layer.</li>
      <li>ReLU is the default; sigmoid saturates and causes vanishing gradients; GELU is used in GPT.</li>
      <li>Universal approximation: one hidden layer can represent anything, but depth is what makes it practical.</li>
      <li>Every transformer block contains this MLP: Linear → GELU → Linear.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>
</Note>
